// The two files that run in a worker rather than on the page.
//
// They need their own configuration because a worker has different globals: no
// `document`, no `window`, and a `self` that is a worker scope rather than the
// page's. Checking them against the page's globals is what let sw.js reach for
// `self.clients` -- which is real in a service worker and nowhere else -- with
// nothing to say whether it was right.
//
// engine/js/blokie.js is pulled in here too, since ai-worker.js imports it.
// That is worth having: the engine is loaded on the page, in this worker, and
// by the tests under Node, so checking it against the worker's globals as well
// is what says it stayed environment-agnostic.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ES2022", "WebWorker", "WebWorker.Iterable"]
  },
  "include": [
    "sw.js",
    "web/ai-worker.js"
  ]
}
